      "observed_sha256": "35a90f25ae3b877f54a20675e951141bf31735460e0aba2efeafb35b27af0e99"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:8:2",
      "id": "thm-ostrowskis-theorem-for-the-rationals",
      "location": "1.2",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The base-p triangle inequality should contain |a_i|, not the ordinary integer a_i: the displayed bound asserts |m| <= sum a_i|p|^i. For the valid absolute value |x|=|x|_infty^10, take p=5 and m=3 (so r=0 and a_0=3); it claims 3^10<=3, which is false. A finite coefficient bound repairs the growth argument.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "0303bbb7c6942b51eb20d95dcf5a996d7df6d757fd45b9a2c93470f80a239743"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:8:3",
      "id": "thm-p-adic-newton-criterion",
      "location": "4.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The hypothesis does not make f'(a_0) a unit, so v congruent to f'(a_0) modulo p does not imply v is a unit. For p=3, f(X)=X^2-9 and a_0=12 satisfy |f(a_0)|_3=3^-3<3^-2=|f'(a_0)|_3^2, while f'(a_0)=24 lies in 3Z_3. Uniqueness can instead follow by proving |v-f'(a_0)|_p<|f'(a_0)|_p, but that argument is absent.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "963e65530a4ac5fd1650ba24bddac48fb0735965311ac44dc29038930079fe9c"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:8:4",
      "id": "thm-weak-approximation-for-rational-places",
      "location": "3.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "With M=product p_i^N_i, M/D need not have p_i-adic valuation at least N_i when p_i divides D. For p=2, D_0=2, T odd, and N=2, D=2T and M/D=2/T has v_2=1<2. Adding kM/D can therefore destroy the precision from step 2.1. Defining M with exponents N_i+v_{p_i}(D) repairs the construction.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "7f827866759d022408e2881d4f78d87ab5e506dbf9ec192c1300d1cf10d8f164"
    }
